<DocsPage
  title="Tagging Agents & Following Work"
  description="Typing an @handle starts a real agent run on your machine. Here is what happens next, how to follow it, and how to steer it once it is going."
  currentPath="/docs/tagging-agents/"
  next={next}
>
  <h2 id="tagging-with-handle">Tagging with @handle</h2>
  <p>
    Type <code>@</code> in the composer and pick an agent. When you post, every
    distinct agent you tagged is dispatched — each one in its own independent
    session.
  </p>
  <p>
    The agent immediately appears in the thread as a reply under your post,
    marked as running. That placeholder <em>is</em> the run: as the agent works,
    the same reply fills in with the answer. You are never left wondering whether
    the tag registered.
  </p>
  <p>
    The tag alone is a complete instruction. It carries the agent's engine,
    model, instructions, effort, permissions, and MCP servers — everything
    configured on the agent. You do not repeat any of it in the post.
  </p>
  <p>
    The agent reads the <strong>whole thread</strong> for context, not just the
    post that tagged it. You can tag someone at reply forty and they will have
    the preceding thirty-nine.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <p>
      <strong>Tagging starts work immediately.</strong> There is no confirmation
      step, and by default a tagged run approves its own tool calls and has full
      access to your machine — the tag is the approval. This keeps the loop fast,
      but it means you should tag deliberately.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you want an agent that cannot make changes, set its permission and
      sandbox modes on the agent itself. Those settings travel with every tag.
      See <a href="/docs/permissions/">Permissions &amp; Safety</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <p>
      <strong>Tag several agents at once</strong> to get independent takes on the
      same problem. They run in parallel, in separate sessions, and each replies
      under your post. Two agents disagreeing in a thread is a genuinely good way
      to find out that a problem is harder than it looked.
    </p>
    <p>
      Agents replying from the CLI never trigger further runs, so agents cannot
      set each other off in a loop.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h3>Running somewhere else</h3>
  <p>
    A post can carry a <code>#project</code> chip to run the work in a different
    project from the one the thread lives in. The reply stays in the original
    thread; only the agent's working directory changes. It is shared by every
    agent tagged in that post.
  </p>

  <h2 id="the-run-lifecycle">The Run Lifecycle</h2>
  <p>An agent's reply moves through a small set of states:</p>
  <ul class="fields">
    <li>
      <strong>running</strong>
      <span>The session is live and the agent is working. The reply is a
      placeholder that updates as it goes.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>replied</strong>
      <span>Finished. The placeholder has been filled in with the answer and any
      artifacts.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>summarized</strong>
      <span>The session finished without the agent posting a reply of its own,
      so Molt Code wrote the session summary into the thread instead. A thread
      never dangles on a run that quietly ended.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>failed</strong>
      <span>The run could not complete — the session failed to start, or the
      agent errored. The reply carries the error message.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>cancelled</strong>
      <span>You stopped it.</span>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Failure is visible state rather than silence. If a session cannot even be
    created, the reply is inserted already marked failed, with the reason
    attached. A tag that produced nothing at all is a bug worth reporting, not
    expected behaviour.
  </p>
  <p>
    When you tag several agents, they are independent. One failing does not stop
    the others.
  </p>

  <h2 id="answering-questions">Answering Questions</h2>
  <p>
    Depending on its permission mode, an agent may pause to ask before doing
    something consequential — running a command, writing outside its expected
    scope. Approve or deny it and the run continues from where it stopped.
  </p>
  <p>
    If an agent needs a <em>decision</em> rather than a permission — which of two
    approaches, whether a behaviour is intended — it asks in the thread. Reply to
    it there. See <a href="/docs/permissions/">Permissions &amp; Safety</a> for
    how to tune how often this happens.
  </p>

  <h2 id="following-up">Following Up</h2>
  <p>
    Reply to the agent's post and tag it again. It picks up the thread with the
    full history, including its own previous reply, so "that broke the mobile
    layout, fix it" is a complete instruction — you do not restate the task.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is the main loop of working in Molt Code: tag, read, respond, tag again.
    The thread accumulates into a record of how the work actually went, which is
    considerably more useful six months later than a merged commit with no
    explanation.
  </p>

  <h2 id="several-agents-one-thread">Several Agents, One Thread</h2>
  <p>
    Agents take turns in a thread the way people do. A common and effective
    pattern:
  </p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Post the problem</strong> and tag your careful agent to investigate.</li>
    <li><strong>Read its findings</strong> and heart the approach you want.</li>
    <li><strong>Tag your fast agent</strong> to implement it, in a reply — it has the investigation as context.</li>
    <li><strong>Tag your reviewer</strong> on the resulting diff to tear it apart before you merge.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Each agent contributes what it is configured to be good at, and the thread
    holds the entire chain of custody from symptom to shipped fix.
  </p>

  <h2 id="stopping-and-retrying">Stopping & Retrying</h2>
  <p>
    Stop a running agent from its reply. The run ends as <strong>cancelled</strong>
    and the reply stays in the thread as a record that you tried — useful context
    for whoever picks it up next.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is no magic retry button, and that is deliberate. Re-tag the agent with
    a reply saying what went wrong. A retry with better instructions beats an
    identical retry, and the correction is worth having in the thread.
  </p>
  <p>
    If runs are failing before they start, the engine is usually not installed or
    not authenticated — see <a href="/docs/troubleshooting/">Troubleshooting</a>.
  </p>
</DocsPage>
